WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.900
How do guests, hosts and

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.120
hypervisors fit together? A virtual

3
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.480
machine presents a machine-like environment

4
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.080
in which a guest operating

5
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.020
system runs applications. A hypervisor,

6
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.760
also called a virtual machine

7
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.320
monitor, controls the relationship between

8
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.040
that environment and the physical

9
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:17.480
host.

10
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.980
Inside a guest, its scheduler

11
00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.640
chooses a guest process to

12
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.140
run on a virtual processor.

13
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.260
The host scheduling layer chooses

14
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.320
when that virtual processor receives

15
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.320
a physical execution slot. Both

16
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.200
scheduling decisions can affect when

17
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.800
the application actually progresses.

18
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.980
Return to sixteen virtual processors

19
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.940
backed by four physical cores.

20
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.560
A guest may regard its

21
00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.100
process as selected for a

22
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.060
virtual processor while that virtual

23
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:44.940
processor waits for host service.

24
00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.680
Distinguish a guest-ready process from

25
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.020
a virtual processor that is

26
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.780
actually executing on hardware.

27
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.720
The conventional Type one diagram

28
00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.080
places the hypervisor directly above

29
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.640
hardware and the guest machines

30
00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.720
above it. The conventional Type

31
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.380
two diagram places a host

32
00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.940
operating system between the virtualization

33
00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.940
application and hardware. Trace the

34
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:07.480
layers shown instead of inferring

35
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.000
classification from an icon.

36
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.900
The course slide labels KVM

37
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.340
with Linux using its Type

38
00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.140
two diagram convention. That example

39
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.100
needs instructor clarification if a

40
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.880
question expects a universal product

41
00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.600
classification. For this course, preserve

42
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.460
the displayed convention while understanding

43
00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.060
the actual layers being discussed.

44
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.960
A process abstraction supplies an

45
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.260
execution context and address space

46
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.300
within an operating system. A

47
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.400
whole virtual machine additionally hosts

48
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.100
an operating system and a

49
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.880
virtualized machine interface. The two

50
00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.920
abstractions operate at different layers

51
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.720
even though both can involve

52
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.080
isolation and scheduling.

53
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.580
The monitor aims to retain

54
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.400
control over the underlying machine

55
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.560
while supporting useful efficiency and

56
00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.720
isolation. A guest kernel can

57
00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:55.800
administer its own environment without

58
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.080
having unrestricted authority over other

59
00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.040
guests or the physical host.

60
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.140
Guest privilege is subordinate to

61
00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.800
the virtualization control layer.

62
00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.300
Isolation is a design goal

63
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.500
with mechanisms and assumptions, not

64
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.180
an automatic consequence of drawing

65
00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.460
separate boxes. The monitor must

66
00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.660
mediate sensitive operations and shared

67
00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:17.500
physical resources. Performance likewise depends

68
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.080
on workload, scheduling and implementation.

69
00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.960
When drawing an answer, label

70
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.080
guest applications, guest operating systems,

71
00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:29.480
virtual processors, the monitor and

72
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.600
the physical hardware. Add a

73
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.480
host operating system where the

74
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.380
chosen architecture requires one. Then

75
00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:37.820
point to the layer responsible

76
00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:40.000
for each dispatch or privileged

77
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:40.700
operation.

78
00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.580
Things to take note of.

79
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.100
Guest processes run on guest

80
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.380
virtual processors, and virtual processors

81
00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.120
need physical service from the

82
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.940
host. Learn the Type one

83
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.540
and Type two layer diagrams,

84
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.540
and keep the slide KVM

85
00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:57.120
example qualified as a course

86
00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:57.640
convention.
